<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
    <artifactId>eyeandsickle-parent</artifactId>
    <version>${revision}</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>eyeandsickle-client</artifactId>
  <name>The Eye and Sickle :: Client</name>
  <description>
    The cross-platform JavaFX desktop client (docs/architecture/01 §1).

    Multi-window by design: one javafx.stage.Stage per tool — map, terminal, rig
    monitor, recon — so the player's screen ends up looking like an operator's
    desk (docs/design/05 §5). A single-window docked fallback is REQUIRED for
    accessibility, not optional (docs/architecture/01 §1, flagged for review).

    The client is a view and input layer. It is never authoritative over
    anything a cheater would forge (Invariant I14). It may mirror server state
    for prediction and it may re-verify provenance chains offline against DID
    public keys (docs/architecture/04 §6.2) — but it decides nothing.
  </description>

  <properties>
    <client.main.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.Launcher</client.main.class>
    <javafx.app.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.EyeAndSickleClient</javafx.app.class>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-protocol</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The single-player runtime. This is what lets the game start with no
      internet, no account and no PostgreSQL.

      It is NOT a back door around the enforcer rules below. `solo` is a plain
      library with no Spring, no driver and no HTTP stack (its own pom enforces
      that), and a character it creates is local-only and can never federate —
      so nothing it produces can enter the economy I14 protects. See
      solo/pom.xml for the full argument.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-solo</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      JavaFX platform natives resolve automatically: org.openjfx's parent pom
      carries profiles keyed on os.name/os.arch that set ${javafx.platform} to
      mac-aarch64 / mac / linux / linux-aarch64 / win, and each artifact pulls
      its own classifier. No os-maven-plugin needed for local development.
      Building for a different OS than the one you are on means pinning the
      classifier explicitly; see the packaging note at the bottom of this file.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-fxml</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Native-feeling theming across macOS / Linux / Windows so the client
         doesn't read as a 2005 Swing app on any of them. -->
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <mainClass>${javafx.app.class}</mainClass>
          <options>
            <!--
              JavaFX loads native graphics libraries via System::load, which JDK
              24+ reports as a restricted-method call and which a future JDK will
              block outright. Granting it explicitly silences the warning now and
              stops the client from breaking on a JDK bump later.
            -->
            <option>--enable-native-access=javafx.graphics</option>
            <!-- What macOS puts in the menu bar and the dock. Without it the running application is
                 called "java", which is the runtime's name rather than the game's. Harmless on
                 Windows and Linux, which ignore the flag. Launcher sets the matching system
                 properties for the platforms that read those instead. -->
            <option>-Xdock:name=EAS uOS Client</option>
          </options>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <manifest>
              <mainClass>${client.main.class}</mainClass>
            </manifest>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>client-is-not-authoritative</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <message>
                    INVARIANT I14. The client must not depend on the server
                    module or on Spring, and must never reach a database driver.
                    Server code on the client classpath is how "the client
                    decided the outcome" bugs start. Talk to the server over
                    REST/WebSocket using types from eyeandsickle-protocol.

                    The absence of a &lt;dependency&gt; element is not
                    self-defending — someone will add one. This rule is.
                  </message>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle:eyeandsickle-server</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework.boot:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.postgresql:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.flywaydb:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.hibernate*:*</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>


  <profiles>
    <!--
      SELF-CONTAINED RUNNABLE JARS, one per platform, all buildable from ONE machine:

          for p in win mac mac-aarch64 linux linux-aarch64; do
            mvn -Pdist -Ddist.platform=$p -pl client -am package -DskipTests
          done

      produces client/target/eyeandsickle-client-<version>-<platform>.jar, each launched with
      `java -jar <file>`. Unlike jpackage this needs no machine of the target OS, because
      nothing is compiled per platform — only a different set of prebuilt JavaFX natives is
      packaged. `dist.platform` defaults to this machine's, so a plain `mvn -Pdist ... package`
      builds the one you can test locally.

      ⚠ WHY NOT ONE JAR FOR EVERYTHING — this was built, measured and rejected, so please do
      not "simplify" it back. JavaFX puts its natives at the JAR ROOT under names that carry
      the OS but NOT the architecture: `libglass.dylib` is the name on both Intel and Apple
      Silicon, and `libglass.so` on both x64 and ARM Linux. Merging all five classifier jars
      therefore has the two Mac builds overwrite each other, and the two Linux builds likewise
      — silently, because shade sees identical paths and keeps the last one. The resulting jar
      really did contain a single x86_64 `libglass.dylib`, and it failed on the arm64 machine
      that built it with:

          UnsatisfiedLinkError ... (have 'x86_64', need 'arm64')

      Windows-x64-plus-Intel-Mac-plus-x64-Linux in one file is achievable and is what that jar
      was. It is not worth having: it excludes every Apple Silicon Mac, which is most of them.

      A true single jar is possible but needs the launcher to extract arch-scoped natives and
      point JavaFX at them through `javafx.cachedir` (whose layout is `<dir>/<runtime version>/
      <os.arch>/`) — internals, undocumented, and silently wrong again if they ever change.
      Per-platform jars cost one loop and depend on nothing.

      ⚠ MUST RUN FROM THE CLASSPATH, which this client already requires: `Launcher` does not
      extend Application precisely so the JVM does not look for JavaFX on the module path before
      main() runs. `java -jar` is a classpath launch, so the two agree rather than fight. Do NOT
      add module-info.java to make this "cleaner"; that recreates the jlink problem below.

      ⚠ DOES NOT bundle a Java runtime — the player needs JDK/JRE 25+ on PATH. That is the
      trade against jpackage, which does bundle one but CANNOT CROSS-COMPILE: a Windows image
      must be built on Windows. This needs one machine; that needs three.

      ⚠ On JDK 24+ a classpath launch warns unless started with the
      `enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` flag (two leading dashes; XML comments cannot contain
      them). It is a warning, not a failure. The `javafx.graphics` form of that flag is WRONG
      here and grants nothing — see CLAUDE.md; this is the one flag that differs by launch mode.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>dist</id>
      <properties>
        <!-- org.openjfx's parent pom sets ${javafx.platform} from os.name/os.arch, so the
             default is whatever this machine is and a local `mvn -Pdist` is testable. -->
        <dist.platform>${javafx.platform}</dist.platform>
      </properties>
      <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-base</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-graphics</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-fxml</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      </dependencies>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <phase>package</phase>
                <goals><goal>shade</goal></goals>
                <configuration>
                  <shadedArtifactAttached>true</shadedArtifactAttached>
                  <shadedClassifierName>${dist.platform}</shadedClassifierName>
                  <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
                  <transformers>
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">
                      <mainClass>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.Launcher</mainClass>
                      <manifestEntries>
                        <!-- Jackson ships multi-release jars; without this the JDK ignores their
                             versioned classes and Jackson fails in ways that look like ours. -->
                        <Multi-Release>true</Multi-Release>
                      </manifestEntries>
                    </transformer>
                    <!-- ServiceLoader files must be CONCATENATED, not overwritten. Jackson 3
                         discovers modules this way, and last-one-wins would drop every provider
                         but one — surfacing as a save that will not deserialise rather than as
                         anything resembling a packaging error. -->
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>
                  </transformers>
                  <filters>
                    <filter>
                      <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                      <excludes>
                        <!-- Several copies of the same descriptor cannot coexist, and a classpath
                             launch never reads them anyway. -->
                        <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                        <!-- Signatures do not survive repackaging: the digests stop matching the
                             contents and the JVM refuses the jar with a SecurityException. -->
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                      </excludes>
                    </filter>
                  </filters>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>

  <!--
    NO SELF-CONTAINED INSTALLER YET — deliberately, and this is worth reading before you
    add one back.

    docs/architecture/01 §1 lists self-contained packaging (jlink/jpackage) as a
    [PROPOSAL] so players never have to "install a JRE first". An earlier draft
    of this pom wired `javafx:jlink` into a `dist` profile. It does not work, and
    it fails for a structural reason rather than a configuration mistake:

      jlink can only link EXPLICIT JPMS modules. This client runs from the
      classpath and has no module-info.java, and — more stubbornly — the
      provenance canonicalizer `io.github.erdtman:java-json-canonicalization`
      is a plain Java 8 jar with no module descriptor, so it can only ever be
      an AUTOMATIC module, and jlink refuses to link automatic modules.

    So the two real options, whenever this is picked up:

      1. jpackage in non-modular app-image mode (`type app-image`, `input <dir>`, `main-jar`). Works today with the classpath layout, produces a bundled
         runtime, needs no module-info anywhere. Largest image, least friction.
         This is the recommended first move.
      2. Full JPMS: add module-info.java to protocol and client AND replace or
         patch the canonicalizer with something modular. Smallest image, but it
         is a real dependency-graph project, not an afternoon.

    Shipping a profile whose documented command fails every time is worse than
    shipping no profile, so there is no profile. `mvn verify` and `mvn
    -pl client javafx:run` are the supported paths until this is built properly.
  -->
</project>
